+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Bandwidth:** Sending the raw node-list to CPU causes enormous bandwidth usage. Compaction is essential [S1].
+- **Precision:** Coordinate updates use integer arithmetic for octant bits to avoid floating-point errors [S2].
+- **Degenerate Input:** If a node has no children (terminal), the subdivision bit should not be set, but the node should still be recorded for caching [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Leaf Node Representation
-A specific variant focusing on the data layout of leaf nodes in the [zazen](https://github.com/gabrielmuller/zazen) implementation [S2, unofficial]. Leaves are represented as 4-byte RGBA blocks. A leaf is considered valid if `rgba[3] != 0x00` [S2, unofficial]. The `set_color(pixel, lightness)` function multiplies the RGB channels by `lightness` but leaves the alpha channel unchanged [S2, unofficial]. Comparison uses bitwise equality of the entire 4-byte block, not element-wise checks [S2, unofficial].
+A specific implementation detail where leaf nodes are represented as 4-byte RGBA blocks [S3].
+- **Validity:** A leaf node is valid if `rgba[3] != 0x00` [S3].
+- **Color Update:** The `set_color(pixel, lightness)` function multiplies RGB channels by lightness but does not modify alpha [S3].
+- **Equality:** Equality checks use bitwise comparison of the entire 4-byte RGBA block, not element-wise comparison [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of nodes in the tree, as every node is visited exactly once. [G]
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(L)$ where $L$ is the number of leaf/terminal nodes, for the result vector. [G]
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; decouples traversal from rendering; efficient for batch processing. [G]
-*   **Limitations:** Requires storing all leaf nodes in memory; not suitable for ray-by-ray rendering where early termination is critical. [G]
-*   **When to use:** When rendering requires all visible geometry (e.g., global illumination, shadow maps) or when parallelizing leaf processing. [G]
-*   **When not to use:** For standard ray marching where early exit on empty space is desired. [G]
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of nodes visited during traversal [S1]. The FIFO limit caps the number of nodes processed per frame.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of nodes in the `node-list` and `fifo`. The FIFO size is bounded (48-96 elements) [S1].
+- **Performance:** Improves reactivity in regions with high node density by ensuring information output in each frame [S1]. No penalization due to temporal coherence [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Efficiently manages data streaming by focusing on nodes actually used in rendering. Reduces CPU-GPU bandwidth via compaction [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Requires additional metadata (subdivision bit) in nodes. Compaction adds CPU overhead [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Leaf Collection is a fundamental technique in hierarchical volume rendering, often used as a preprocessing step. [G] The specific implementation details cited here are derived from open-source repositories [isomesh](https://github.com/narnia-ai-mason/isomesh) and [zazen](https://github.com/gabrielmuller/zazen). [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]
+Leaf Collection is part of the Ray-Guided Streaming framework for efficient and detailed voxel rendering [S1]. It was introduced to address the challenge of keeping the CPU informed about which parts of the sparse voxel octree need to be streamed or updated, leveraging both spatial and temporal coherence [S1].
 
